hw/sim/ - GHDL testbenches
==========================

Self-checking VHDL-2008 testbenches. Each asserts expected values and reports
via std.env; a failed assertion fails `make -f hw.mk sim`.

    tb_pe.vhd         directed PE checks for arithmetic, accumulation, shift,
                      signed comparison, constants and register hold.
    tb_uart.vhd       loops uart_tx.tx_serial back into uart_rx and checks that
                      adversarial byte patterns survive framing + mid-bit
                      sampling bit-exact. Has a watchdog that fails on a stall.
    tb_cgra_top.vhd   full-chip: feeds real UART byte streams into cgra_top
                      and checks CFG/WR/RUN/RD/RST/EXEC, checksums and NACKs at
                      the wire level. This is the protocol conformance test.
                      The EXEC cases cover a masked read-back with a fused
                      reset, an empty tap mask (pure fused write+run), and the
                      guarantee that a corrupted EXEC payload returns a
                      full-length reply with NACK *without* stepping the array.
    tb_matvec.vhd     exercises the weight-stationary systolic matrix-vector
                      mapping end to end. Parametric on G_STEP_DIV (the datapath
                      multicycle factor): the same result must hold at any
                      cadence, e.g.
                        ghdl -r --std=08 --workdir=build/ghdl tb_matvec \
                             -gG_STEP_DIV=3 --assert-level=error
                      proves one step = one result independent of G_STEP_DIV.
    tb_array_diff.vhd compares all 16 RTL PE outputs against values generated
                      by libcgra's C emulator for 64 deterministic full-array
                      configurations: every opcode with all 8x8 operand-select
                      pairs, 1-4 steps, and samples including signed extremes.
                      The generated vector file lives in build/.
    tb_protocol_diff.vhd
                      replays 504 transactions generated from emu.c against
                      cgra_ctrl + cgra_array, comparing every reply byte under
                      variable transmit backpressure. Includes all commands,
                      0/1/2/7/255 steps, single/full/sparse/empty EXEC masks,
                      reset and accumulation, split CFG packets, and bad
                      CFG/WR/EXEC checksums followed by RUN/RD. Runs at
                      STEP_DIV=1,2,3,4, with assertions on actual step spacing.
                      Also runs raw transcripts and real host kernel traces
                      on 1x1, 1x16, 16x1, 2x3, 3x2, 2x8, 8x2 and 4x4.
                      Timed watchdog recovery is checked
                      separately in RTL (the C emulator has no clock).
    tb_array_geometry.vhd
                      verifies 1x1, 1x4, 4x1, 2x3, 3x2, 4x4 and 8x8 arrays:
                      both operand muxes, all neighbour/edge connections,
                      one-hop-per-step propagation, hold, and reset priority.
                      The standalone 8x8 array exceeds the current protocol
                      limit of 16 PEs and is not a full-device configuration.
    tb_kernel_uart.vhd
                      replays real libcgra + DSL kernel requests through the
                      complete UART top-level, on 2x3 and 3x2.
    gen_kernel_vectors.c
                      records serial traffic through a POSIX PTY while the
                      real host kernels check independent scalar references.
                      Shares the workload in sw/test/kernel_cases.h.

All are self-checking (assert ... severity failure + std.env.finish), so a
failure fails `make sim`; there is nothing to eyeball in a waveform.

Run
---
    make -f hw.mk sim     # run all benches, differential checks and shape sweep
    make -f hw.mk wave    # tb_cgra_top -> build/tb_cgra_top.ghw (gtkwave)
    make -f hw.mk sim DEVICE_SHAPES="2x3 3x2" UART_SHAPES="2x3"
    make -f hw.mk sim MESH_SHAPES="2x4 4x2" STEP_DIVS="2 3"

Simulation needs GHDL, a C11 compiler and POSIX pseudo-terminals. Vector generators
compile the current emulator sources in build/ghdl, independent of the host
library's build/profile. A failed generator cannot replace a valid vector file.

The host-side emulator (lib/src/emu.c) is the golden reference for the same
semantics: if a testbench and the emulator disagree, one of them is wrong.
